Exercise promotes the growth of new neurons in the hippocampus, which is crucial for memory and learning, directly enhancing cognitive function.
Studies show that regular physical activity improves attention and focus, supporting better cognitive performance in daily tasks.
The release of neurotransmitters like serotonin and dopamine during exercise helps regulate mood, reducing symptoms of depression and anxiety.
Neurogenesis and improved brain health from exercise have been linked to a lower risk of neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer's.
Clinical trials have demonstrated that exercise interventions can significantly improve mood and cognitive abilities in individuals with depression and cognitive impairments.